          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A quantifier is an operation in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
            that moves a statement from one
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
            to a related context.
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %h3#quantification_in_firstorder_logic Quantification in first-order logic
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first-order+logic"} first-order logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicate"} predicate
            on one type to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proposition"} proposition
            (a predicate on no types), or more generally moves a predicate on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n + 1
            types to a predicate on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            types.
          %p
            Given a predicate
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            on a type
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+quantification"} universal quantification
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∀
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi T
                  %mo ,
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall\, x\colon T, P(x)
            (and with many variations in punctuation), is intended to be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/true"} true
            if and only if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(a)
            is true for every possible element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/existential+quantification"} existential quantification
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            (also called its
            = succeed ")," do
              %strong particular quantification
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∃
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi T
                    %mo ,
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists\, x\colon T, P(x)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(a)
            is true for at least one element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(a)
            may be provable (in a given context
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            %em term
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            of type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            that
            %em can actually be constructed
            (in
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∀
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi T
                  %mo ,
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall\, x\colon T, P(x)
            cannot be proved; conversely, it is quite possible that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∃
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi T
                  %mo ,
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists\, x\colon T, P(x)
            can be proved (in a given context) but
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(a)
            cannot be proved for any term
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            of type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            that can actually be constructed.
          %p Therefore, we must define the quantifiers more carefully; one way to do this is as follows:
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                    %msub
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi Γ
                    %mo ∀
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi T
                    %mo ,
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q \vdash_{\Gamma}\forall\, x\colon T, P(x)
              if and only if
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Q
                      %msub
                        %mo ⊢
                        %mrow
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo ,
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                          %mi T
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q \vdash_{\Gamma, x\colon T} P(x)
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∃
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi T
                    %mo ,
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi Γ
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists\, x\colon T, P(x) \vdash_{\Gamma} Q
              if and only if
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %msub
                        %mo ⊢
                        %mrow
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo ,
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                          %mi T
                      %mi Q
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(x) \vdash_{\Gamma, x\colon T} Q
          %p
            Here,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            is an arbitrary context, and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma, x\colon T
            is that context supplemented by a free variable
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            of type
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            is (a priori) a statement in the context
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            but also needs to make sense in the context
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma, x\colon T
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weakening+rule"} weakening rule
            for this to make sense. This definition also presumes that a statement (in any given context) can be identified precisely by placing it within the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
            of statements (in that context) under entailment (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊢
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vdash
            ), which is true for ordinary first-order logic.
          %p
            From this definition, one can derive rules for the quantifiers in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequent+calculus"} sequent calculus
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+deduction"} natural deduction
          %h3#quantification_in_firstorder_logic_with_equality Quantification in first-order logic with equality
          %p
            If all of our types have an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality+predicate"} equality predicate
          %p
            Given a predicate
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            on a type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            with equality, the
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/uniqueness+quantifier"} uniqueness quantifier
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∃
                    %mo !
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi T
                    %mo ,
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists!\, x\colon T, P(x)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∃
                  %mo !
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi T
                  %mo ,
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≡
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ∃
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi T
                  %mo ,
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∧
                  %mo ∀
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi T
                  %mo ,
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo =
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists!\, x\colon T, P(x) \;\equiv\; \exists\, x\colon T, P(x) \wedge \forall\, y\colon T, P(y) \Rightarrow (x = y) .
          %p
            The intended interpretation is that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∃
                  %mo !
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi T
                  %mo ,
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists!\, x\colon T, P(x)
            is true iff
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(a)
            is true for
            %em exactly
            one element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
          %p
            Given a natural number
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            holds for exactly
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            elements, at most
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            elements, at least
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            elements, etc. (These all require
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/negation"} negation
            and therefore there are some slight variations possible when using
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+logic"} intuitionistic logic
          %p
            With these examples, one can see quite readily that quantification is about saying how often a statement is true, that is
            %em quantifying
            it.
          %h3#LawvereQuantifier Lawvere quantifiers: Quantification as adjunction
          %p
            Given two contexts
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
            and an interpretation
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo →
                    %mi Δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon \Gamma \to \Delta
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msub
                    %mi Prop
                    %mi Δ
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi Prop
                    %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^*\colon Prop_\Delta \to Prop_\Gamma
            from the propositions in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
            to the propositions in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+quantifier"} universal quantifier
            along
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/existential+quantifier"} existential quantifier
            along
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is given by weakening.
          %p
            To bring this down to earth, let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            and let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi S
                  %mo →
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon S \to T
            be a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsets"} subsets
            of the corresponding set. In this way, we are looking at
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi 𝒫
                    %mi T
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝒫
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^*\colon \mathcal{P}T \to \mathcal{P}S
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preimage"} preimage
            map between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+sets"} power sets
            (often denoted
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{-1}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall_f
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∃
                    %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists_f
            are maps from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒫
                  %mi S
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝒫
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}S \to \mathcal{P}T
            as follows:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ∀
                        %mi f
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi A
                      %mo ≔
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi T
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ∀
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi S
                      %mo ,
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi y
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall_f\, A \coloneqq \{ y\colon T \;|\; \forall\, x\colon S,\; f(x) = y \;\Rightarrow\; x \in A \}
                ;
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ∃
                        %mi f
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi A
                      %mo ≔
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi T
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ∃
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi S
                      %mo ,
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi y
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ∧
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists_f\, A \coloneqq \{ y\colon T \;|\; \exists\, x\colon S,\; f(x) = y \;\wedge\; x \in A \}
                \.
          %p
            Note that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∃
                    %mi f
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists_f A
            is simply the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/image"} image
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            restricted to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∃
                    %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists_f
            as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_!
            (if not simply
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall_f
            as
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_*
          %p
            When
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is the unique function from a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to the terminal set,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒫
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}1
            is the two-element set and an object in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒫
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}X
            is a predicate on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            to a truth value:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∃
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists x \in X
                such that
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Q
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q(x)
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∀
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Q
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall x \in X, Q(x).
          %h3#Guarded Guarded quantification: quantifying over subtypes
          %p
            Recall that, given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            and a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicate"} predicate
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subtype"} subtype
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            defined by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            is, or would be, a type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            whose elements (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terms"}> terms
            )
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            are thought of as elements (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terms"}> terms
            ) of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q(x)
            holds. But many
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theories"} type theories
            do not include sub-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+formation"} type formation
            among their rules. However, we can mimic quantification over subtypes by using so-called ‘guarded’ quantifiers.
          %p
            Specifically, given a type
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %strong guarded quantifications
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            relative to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            are these
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions"} propositions
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %munder
                        %mo ∀
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                          %mi T
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                      %mi Q
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\underset{x\colon T}{\forall}
                      \\big( Q(x) \Rightarrow P(x) \big)
                ,
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %munder
                        %mo ∃
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                          %mi T
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                      %mi Q
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\underset{x\colon T}{\exists}
                      \\big( Q(x) \wedge P(x) \big)
                \.
          %p
            We have already had an example of these on this page, in the discussion of the Lawvere quantifiers along a function (
            %a{:href => "#LawvereQuantifier"}> above
            ).
          %p
            When the notation for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            makes the type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            obvious, and especially when the variable appears only at beginning in this notation, it is quite common to suppress mention of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            and write “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∀
                  %mi Q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall Q(x), P(x)
            ” and “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∃
                  %mi Q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists Q(x), P(x)
            ”. This is particularly common in untyped theories where the domain type “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            ” would be notationally suppressed anyway; for example, this is almost always how one writes guarded quantifiers in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material+set+theory"} material set theory
          %h3#bounded_quantification_size_issues_and_predicativity Bounded quantification: Size issues and predicativity
          %p
            When
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            is used as a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundation+of+mathematics"} foundation of mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universe"} universe
            of types (see also at
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+of+types"} type of types
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            is used as a foundation, we have a type of all sets and can consider quantification over this type. Such quantification is called
            %strong unbounded
            and often forbidden in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+separation"} axiom of separation
            (if it can be stated in the language at all), especially in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicative+mathematics"} predicative mathematics
            but also in some impredicative foundations, such as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+classes"} proper classes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SEAR"} SEAR
            (although it is allowed in Morse–Kelley class theory).
          %p
            In contrast, quantification over variables from
            %em small
            types (for a suitable notion of smallness) is called
            = succeed "." do
              %strong bounded quantification
          %p
            In the usual formulations of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material+set+theory"} material set theory
          %p
            In classical mathematics, at least, formulas with unbounded quantifiers can be classified into the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L%C3%A9vy+hierarchy"} Lévy hierarchy
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Δ
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_0
            consists of the formulas with only bounded quantifiers.
          %h3#internalised_quantifiers Internalised quantifiers
          %p
            …
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+category"} Heyting category
            …
          %h3#categorified_quantifiers Categorified quantifiers
          %p
            …
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+product"} dependent product
            …
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+sum"} dependent sum
            …
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/existential+quantifier"}> existential quantifier
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+sum"} dependent sum
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+quantifier"}> universal quantifier
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+product"} dependent product
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/uniqueness+quantifier"} uniqueness quantifier
          %h2#RelatedConcepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+dicto+and+de+re"} de dicto and de re
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L%C3%A9vy+hierarchy"} Lévy hierarchy
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elimination+of+quantifiers"} elimination of quantifiers
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+quantifier"} generalized quantifier
          %h2#references References
          %p General linguistical and philosophical discussion is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/quantification"} Quantifiers and quantification
              in Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy
          %p
            The identification of universal/existential quantification with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoints"} adjoints
            of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/substitution"} substitution
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context+extension"} context extension
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+product"}> dependent product
            \/
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+sum"}> dependent sum
            ) originates around
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Adjointness in Foundations
                %strong 23
                (1969) 281-296,
              %p
                reprinted in: Reprints in Theory and Applications of Categories
                %strong 16
                (2006) 1-16 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://www.emis.de/journals/TAC/reprints/articles/16/tr16abs.html"}> tac:tr16
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.emis.de/journals/TAC/reprints/articles/16/tr16.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Quantifiers and sheaves
                %strong 1
                (1970) 329-334 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mattearnshaw/lawvere/master/pdfs/1970-quantifiers-and-sheaves.pdf"}> pdf
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Lawvere-QuantifiersAndSheaves.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p A definition of guarded and bounded quantification for the membership relation could be found in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Shulman"} Michael Shulman
              (2018). Comparing material and structural set theories.
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.05204"} arXiv:1808.05204
        .revisedby
